From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PATCH] ptrace, proc: a thread may reach its own process's /proc/self/fd

Backport of mainline 96d0df79f264 ("proc: make proc_fd_permission()
thread-friendly") and 73af963f9f30 ("ptrace: __ptrace_may_access() should
not deny sub-threads"), both from 3.9. Each replaces a comparison against
the current *task* with one against the current *thread group*, on the
reasoning Oleg Nesterov gives in the second: between tasks that share an
->mm there is nothing to protect, so a check that distinguishes them is not
security, only a surprise.

Without them an application cannot be installed on this device at all. The
path is the same for adb install, for the package installer and for this
port's WeRead entry, and it ends here:

    PackageInstallerSession.openWriteInternal()
      Libcore.os.open(target, O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, 0644)
      StorageManager.allocateBytes(fd, lengthBytes, ...)    // size declared
        ParcelFileDescriptor.getFile(fd)
          Os.readlink("/proc/self/fd/" + fd)   -> EACCES

and the caller reports "failed to write; readlink failed: EACCES
(Permission denied)" (PW3 Android, V80 on the device, 2026-10-23;
reproduced outside the port's own code with `cmd package install-write -S`).
The install paths that do not declare a size skip allocateBytes and have
always worked, which is why this went unseen until the WeRead entry, which
knows the length, first reached its install step.

Two checks deny it, and both are in these two commits:

  * system_server changes uid at startup, so commit_creds() sets it not
    dumpable, and pid_revalidate() therefore gives every /proc/<pid> inode
    to root rather than to the process (the device shows `dr-x------ root
    root /proc/1281/fd`). A binder thread of uid 1000 fails
    generic_permission() on that directory, and proc_fd_permission()'s
    second chance, `task_pid(current) == proc_pid(inode)`, is the thread's
    own pid against the group leader's -- false for every thread but one.

  * Had it got that far, proc_pid_readlink() asks
    proc_fd_access_allowed() -> ptrace_may_access(), whose first line is
    `if (task == current) return 0;` -- again the leader, not this thread.
    It then reaches `if (!dumpable && !task_ns_capable(task,
    CAP_SYS_PTRACE))` and returns -EPERM, which proc_pid_readlink() reports
    as -EACCES.

Android 8.1 is written against kernels that carry both commits, which is
why this is a 3.0 problem and not an AOSP one: the framework may ask a
worker thread to readlink a write-only descriptor of its own process, and
on any kernel from 3.9 onwards it may.

Adapted to 3.0: proc_fd_permission() still takes the RCU-walk `flags` and
passes NULL for the ACL hook, and ptrace.c's local is `dumpable` rather
than mainline's later form. Nesting rcu_read_lock() inside an RCU-walk is
allowed; the function's existing habit of turning a -ECHILD from
generic_permission() into 0 for the owning process is left as it is, which
is what the pinned tree already does for the leader.

--- a/fs/proc/base.c
+++ b/fs/proc/base.c
@@ -2111,11 +2111,19 @@
  */
 static int proc_fd_permission(struct inode *inode, int mask, unsigned int flags)
 {
-	int rv = generic_permission(inode, mask, flags, NULL);
+	struct task_struct *p;
+	int rv;
+
+	rv = generic_permission(inode, mask, flags, NULL);
 	if (rv == 0)
 		return 0;
-	if (task_pid(current) == proc_pid(inode))
+
+	rcu_read_lock();
+	p = pid_task(proc_pid(inode), PIDTYPE_PID);
+	if (p && same_thread_group(p, current))
 		rv = 0;
+	rcu_read_unlock();
+
 	return rv;
 }

--- a/kernel/ptrace.c
+++ b/kernel/ptrace.c
@@ -146,7 +146,7 @@
 	 */
 	int dumpable = 0;
 	/* Don't let security modules deny introspection */
-	if (task == current)
+	if (same_thread_group(task, current))
 		return 0;
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	tcred = __task_cred(task);
